UPTIME(1)                      P32W Unix                       UPTIME(1)

NAME
       uptime - how long the system has been up

SYNOPSIS
       uptime

DESCRIPTION
       uptime prints one line: the time of day (local, see tz(5)),
       how long since the system booted (days, then hours:minutes),
       how many users are logged in (records in /etc/utmp), and the
       load average: the mean number of runnable processes over
       the last 1, 5 and 15 minutes, from sysinfo(2).

EXAMPLES
       $ uptime
        10:42:07 up  3:15, 2 users,  load average: 0.08, 0.03, 0.01

       After a day the up time reads, e.g., up 2 days,  3:15.

FILES
       /etc/utmp      the logged-in users

EXIT STATUS
       0.

SEE ALSO
       top(1), who(1), ps(1), date(1), sysinfo(2), times(2)

BUGS
       There are no options. If sysinfo(2) fails the load average
       is left out. A user logged in on both terminals counts
       twice.
